.TH PNPSIM "9" "2026-09-08" "LinuxCNC Documentation" "HAL Component"
.SH NAME

pnpsim \- Simulated pick-and-place gripper and fixture, for driving pnptask without hardware
.SH SYNOPSIS
.HP
.B load pnpsim [pnpsim.\fIN\fB]
.SH DESCRIPTION


One instance simulates the two field devices a \fBpnptask\fR station pair needs:
a \fBgripper\fR (a picker's close command and its opened/closed feedback) and a
\fBfixture\fR (a process station's release request and its released feedback).
Both halves are independent \(em wire the ones you need and leave the rest
unlinked. A machine with two pickers and two process stations therefore loads
two instances:

.IP
.nf
load pnpsim <pnp.sim.0,pnp.sim.1>
addf pnp.sim.0 servo-thread
addf pnp.sim.1 servo-thread

net p0-close  pnp.task.picker.0.close  => pnp.sim.0.gripper.close
net p0-opened pnp.sim.0.gripper.opened => pnp.task.picker.0.opened
net p0-closed pnp.sim.0.gripper.closed => pnp.task.picker.0.closed
.fi

.PP
The gripper models the one thing a pick-and-place task cannot be tested without:
the difference between closing onto a part and closing onto nothing. Feedback
follows the command after \fBgripper.settle-time\fR, and lands on one of two
states \(em \fBopened\fR high (released), or \fBopened\fR low with
\fBclosed\fR saying what it found: low means the jaws stopped on a part, high
means they met each other and the position was empty.

.PP
Which of the two a close produces is scripted with \fBgripper.miss-count\fR:
0 (the default) grips a part every time, N makes the next N closes find nothing,
and \-1 makes every close find nothing. The counter reloads only when the
pin's \fIvalue changes\fR \(em a pin is a level, so writing the value it
already carries is invisible. To re-arm a fresh run of the same length, write 0
first and then the count. \fBgripper.miss-left\fR reports what is left of the
current run.

.PP
\fBgripper.stuck\fR and \fBfixture.stuck\fR freeze the feedback where it
stands, which is how the fault paths are reached: a gripper that never actuates
raises PICKER_CLOSE_FAILED in pnptask, a fixture that never answers raises
RELEASE_TIMEOUT.

.SH FUNCTIONS
.TP
\fBpnpsim.\fIN\fB\fR (requires a floating-point thread)
Advance both simulations by one servo period.
.SH PINS
.TP
.B pnpsim.\fIN\fB.gripper.close\fR bit in \fR
Close command, from the picker's \fBclose\fR output.
.TP
.B pnpsim.\fIN\fB.gripper.opened\fR bit out \fR(default: \fI1\fR) \fR
Opened feedback. High while the gripper is
released; the initial state.
.TP
.B pnpsim.\fIN\fB.gripper.closed\fR bit out \fR
Fully-closed feedback: high when the jaws met each
other, which after a pick means the position held nothing.
.TP
.B pnpsim.\fIN\fB.gripper.holding\fR bit out \fR
High while the gripper holds a part.
.TP
.B pnpsim.\fIN\fB.gripper.miss-count\fR s32 in \fR(default: \fI0\fR) \fR
How many of the next closes grip nothing:
0 = none, N = the next N, \-1 = all of them. Reloaded whenever the value
changes.
.TP
.B pnpsim.\fIN\fB.gripper.miss-left\fR s32 out \fR
Closes still scheduled to grip nothing.
.TP
.B pnpsim.\fIN\fB.gripper.stuck\fR bit in \fR
Freeze the gripper feedback where it stands \(em a
gripper with no air on it.
.TP
.B pnpsim.\fIN\fB.gripper.settle-time\fR float in \fR(default: \fI0.05\fR) \fR
Seconds between a close/open command
and the feedback that answers it.
.TP
.B pnpsim.\fIN\fB.fixture.release\fR bit in \fR
Release/unclamp request, from the station's \fBrelease\fR output.
.TP
.B pnpsim.\fIN\fB.fixture.released\fR bit out \fR
Released feedback; follows the request after \fBfixture.release-time\fR.
.TP
.B pnpsim.\fIN\fB.fixture.stuck\fR bit in \fR
Freeze the released feedback where it stands.
.TP
.B pnpsim.\fIN\fB.fixture.release-time\fR float in \fR(default: \fI0.02\fR) \fR
Seconds the fixture takes to follow its request.
